// @check
// @error {[string]: i32} isn't equatable — a map has no defined value equality: its entries are insertion-ORDERED and observable that way (`for k in m`, `m.keys()`), so `==` would have to pick between order-sensitive and set-like and neither is what both callers mean — compare `m.size`, or the values at the keys you name
// @error {[string]: i32} | null isn't equatable — a map has no defined value equality: its entries are insertion-ORDERED and observable that way (`for k in m`, `m.keys()`), so `==` would have to pick between order-sensitive and set-like and neither is what both callers mean — compare `m.size`, or the values at the keys you name
// CONTRACT (must-error): the SOUNDNESS BOUNDARY of `==` in the other direction. A rep the
// emitter has no compare core for must not pass `vl check` — `eqCmpKindOfTy` answers "none"
// and the checker rejects, off the same function the emitter's dispatch reads. Every shape
// this file has ever held was `vl check`-CLEAN INVALID WASM before it: no arm claimed it, so
// the `==` fell through to the i32 tail and emitted `i32.eq` over two refs.
//
// **SEVEN SHAPES GRADUATED OUT OF HERE, WHICH IS WHAT THIS FILE'S OWN CONTRACT ASKED FOR.**
// It used to read: "A later change that gives one of these a core must GRADUATE it out of
// this file with a LOG-mismatch pin, not quietly let it become invalid wasm again." D751/D752
// gave `f64[]`, `i64[]`, `f32[]`, a STRUCT-element list, `string[][]`, `i32[][][]` and the
// `f64[] | null` niche a core — `emitListEqGCore`, one loop parameterised on the (wrapper,
// backing) pair plus an element rung — and every one of them is now pinned BY VALUE in
// `arrays/list-eq-every-rep.vl`, three questions each (equal → true, unequal → false, the
// same unequal pair through `!=` → true).
//
// WHAT IS LEFT IS NOT A MISSING CORE. A MAP is refused because the language has no answer for
// what `m1 == m2` MEANS: its entries are insertion-ordered and that order is observable, so
// `==` must choose between order-sensitive and set-like, and both are permanent. That is a
// DOMAIN rule, and the sentence now says so — off the `unsupported-lowering` channel, with no
// concession that the backend merely has not caught up. `silent-class-inventory` D754 carries
// the ruling and the five questions a re-opening has to answer first;
// `selfhost_native_diag_code_test.ts` pins the channel.
//
// THE TWO SPELLINGS DISAGREED BEFORE THIS, AND THAT IS WHY IT WAS A RULING RATHER THAN A
// REWORD. `{[string]: i32}[] == …` printed the SOUNDNESS sentence (`isEquatable` recurses into
// an array element and refuses a map there) while the BARE `m1 == m2` printed the capability
// one, because `eqRefusals`' structural gate fires only on a `TyObj`/`TyArray` operand. One
// program said the design forbids this and its one-constructor neighbour said the backend had
// not caught up, about the same value.
export function eqMap(a: {[string]: i32}, b: {[string]: i32}) { print(a == b) }
export function eqNulMap(a: {[string]: i32} | null, b: {[string]: i32} | null) {
  print(a == b)
}
